      "provenance": {
        "notes": "Not a true fern. The Sprengeri asparagus fern (Asparagus densiflorus) is a different plant often confused with it and sold under the same names. Wiry spiny stems: wear gloves. Care from NC State (full page) and RHS, which agree on bright indirect light, moist well-drained mix, regular water in the growing season and less in winter. NC State: 55 to 70 F, high humidity (no figure), 2 to 3 ft as a houseplant, rapid growth. RHS lists it as a climber needing support, up to 2.5 m. Toxicity: NC State rates it medium severity (berries; abdominal pain, vomiting, diarrhoea; sapogenins; sap irritates skin) and tags it a problem for cats, dogs and children; RHS says fruits harmful if eaten and a skin allergen. The ASPCA page read is for Asparagus densiflorus 'Sprengeri' (toxic to dogs and cats, allergic dermatitis, gastric upset from berries) so it is a related-species reading used only for the pet skin lines. Pets and children graded 2 from 'medium' (agent's mapping); review."
      },
